  Derivation notes (which numbers are MEASURED vs DERIVED):
    MEASURED  : every tally, every wall clock, every per-write percentile above.
    MEASURED  : the fsyncSync tally, hence the number of writes that paid a barrier.
    MEASURED  : the delta scope the CLI itself printed for each one-file stage
                (`updated N file(s) [scope M]`) — which is why one file is not one file.
    DERIVED   : `added ms` is a PRODUCT (count x per-write cost), never a direct timing of
                the workload. Two products are given and they disagree on purpose:
                  - PRESCRIBED FORMULA (renames x per-write cost) is an UPPER BOUND. It
                    charges a barrier to every rename, including the ~93% of renames that
                    pay no barrier at all. It is NOT the cost.
                  - BARRIER-ATTRIBUTABLE (fsyncs x per-barrier cost) charges only the
                    barriers the tally actually observed. This is the honest figure.
                If the two are far apart, the rename count is NOT a proxy for the flush
                count and any capacity argument must use the second product.
    MEASURED  : WHICH call site each rename came from (see the attribution table per
                workload) and, from it, the split between renames that went through
                writeJsonAtomic and renames that bypassed it. The unflushed renames are
                therefore identified, not inferred — an earlier draft of this probe
                asserted "the unflushed renames are the dossiers" from the tally shape
                alone, and a source scan falsified it: `renameSync` with no `fsync` also
                occurs in graph-layout.ts, materialize.ts, freshness-service.ts,
                intelligence-projections.ts, registry.ts and others, so the tally shape
                could not distinguish them. The attribution table is what settles it.
    DERIVED   : `per-barrier cost` splits the measured overhead in two because each durable
                write pays two flushes. The two-barriers-per-write model is CROSS-CHECKED
                against the attribution (renames through writeJsonAtomic vs fsyncSync/2)
                and against the no-op floor, where renames == fsyncSync/2 == 5 and
                unflushedRenames == 0.
    NOT MEASURED: the wall-clock DELTA the barriers add. Nothing here re-times a workload with
                the flushes disabled; the added-ms figures are products, not A/B timings.
    NOT MEASURED: the WP2 watch -> queryable path itself. The gate was recorded against a
                different code path AND a different workload than the CLI mutations measured
                here (a full index of this project takes ~376 s, so the 1956.6 ms gate figure
                cannot be this workload). The percentages above are ADDITIVE BUDGET
                estimates, not a re-measurement of that gate.
    NOT MEASURED: writes that do not rename at all. Only rename/write/fsync/open are counted,
                so a writer that appends (appendLineDurable) or that writes in place would be
                invisible here except through `appendFileSync`/`openSync`. `appendFileSync` is
                0 in every workload below, so `appendLineDurable` was NOT exercised by these
                paths — that is an absence in the workload, not proof it never flushes.

